# Quantitative microbiome profiling disentangles inflammation- and bile duct obstruction-associated microbiota alterations across PSC/IBD diagnoses

## abstract
Recent work has highlighted the importance of confounder control in microbiome association studies1,2. For instance, multiple pathologies previously linked to gut ecosystem dysbiosis display concomitant changes in stool consistency3-6, a major covariate of microbiome variation2,7. In those cases, observed microbiota alterations could largely reflect variation in faecal water content. Moreover, stool moisture variation has been linked to fluctuations in faecal microbial load, inducing artefacts in relative abundance profile analyses8,9. Hence, the identification of associations between the gut microbiota and specific disease manifestations in pathologies with complex aetiologies requires a deconfounded, quantitative assessment of microbiome variation. Here, we revisit a disease association microbiome data set comprising 106 patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) and/or inflammatory bowel disease10. Assessing quantitative taxon abundances9, we study microbiome alterations beyond symptomatic stool moisture variation. We observe an increased prevalence of a low cell count Bacteroides 2 enterotype across the pathologies studied, with microbial loads correlating inversely with intestinal and systemic inflammation markers. Quantitative analyses allow us to differentiate between taxa associated with either intestinal inflammation severity (Fusobacterium) or cholangitis/biliary obstruction (Enterococcus) among previously suggested PSC marker genera. We identify and validate a near-exclusion pattern between the inflammation-associated Fusobacterium and Veillonella genera, with Fusobacterium detection being restricted to Crohn's disease and patients with PSC-Crohn's disease. Overall, through absolute quantification and confounder control, we single out clear-cut microbiome markers associated with pathophysiological manifestations and disease diagnosis.

Recent work has highlighted the importance of confounder control in microbiome association studies1,2. For instance, multiple pathologies previously linked to gut ecosystem dysbiosis display concomitant changes in stool consistency3–6, a major covariate of microbiome variation2,7. In those cases, observed microbiota alterations could largely reflect variation in faecal water content. Moreover, stool moisture variation has been linked to fluctuations in faecal microbial load, inducing artefacts in relative abundance profile analyses8,9. Hence, the identification of associations between the gut microbiota and specific disease manifestations in pathologies with complex aetiologies requires a deconfounded, quantitative assessment of microbiome variation. Here, we revisit a disease association microbiome data set comprising 106 patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) and/or inflammatory bowel disease10. Assessing quantitative taxon abundances9, we study microbiome alterations beyond symptomatic stool moisture variation. We observe an increased prevalence of a low cell count Bacteroides 2 enterotype across the pathologies studied, with microbial loads correlating inversely with intestinal and systemic inflammation markers. Quantitative analyses allow us to differentiate between taxa associated with either intestinal inflammation severity (Fusobacterium) or cholangitis/biliary obstruction (Enterococcus) among previously suggested PSC marker genera. We identify and validate a near-exclusion pattern between the inflammation-associated Fusobacterium and Veillonella genera, with Fusobacterium detection being restricted to Crohn’s disease and patients with PSC–Crohn’s disease. Overall, through absolute quantification and confounder control, we single out clear-cut microbiome markers associated with pathophysiological manifestations and disease diagnosis.
## Methods
Sample collection. Ethical compliance. All experimental protocols were approved by the Commissie Medische Ethiek, UZ KU Leuven (reference numbers: S53684 and S58125). The study design complied with all relevant ethical regulations, aligning with the Declaration of Helsinki and in accordance with Belgian privacy. All participants gave their informed consent.
Study cohort. The study cohort was assembled in the framework of a previous study including patients with PSC, IBD (UC or CD) or PSC with concomitant IBD (PSC–IBD, which include PSC–UC and PSC–CD) and mHcs as described below10. Two additional abbreviations are used to refer to group sets, namely PSC/IBD (which includes PSC, IBD and PSC–IBD) and PSC/IBD/mHC (which includes PSC/IBD and mHCs). In brief, faecal samples were collected from patients with PSC, IBD or PSC–IBD attending the IBD or hepatology outpatient clinic of the University Hospitals Leuven (Leuven, Belgium). The diagnosis of PSC and IBD was based on established guidelines (symptoms, endoscopy, imaging and histology)10. In short, the diagnosis of IBD was based on European Crohn and Colitis Organization (ECCO) guidelines23,24. All patients with IBD were subjected to endoscopy at diagnosis. Disease activity was assessed based on established clinical scores (Mayo score for UC and Harvey Bradshaw index for CD). In addition, serum CRP and faecal calprotectin levels were also used to establish disease activity. The diagnosis of PSC was based on symptoms and/ or signs of chronic cholestatic liver injury, imaging compatible with bile duct injury (magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography and endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) and/or liver biopsy with typical findings, according to the American College of Gastroenterology guidelines25. For patients with PSC or PSC–IBD, age-, gender- and body mass index-matched healthy controls (mHCs) were selected from the FGFP database2.
Validation cohort. Patients with UC or CD $\scriptstyle\cdot n=224$ ) were recruited independently at UZ KU Leuven. Patients were all endoscopically active, as assessed based on established clinical scores (Mayo score for UC and Simple Endoscopic Score for CD (SES-CD) for CD).
Faecal moisture content. Moisture content was determined as the percentage of mass loss after lyophilization from $0.2\mathrm{g}$ frozen aliquots of non-homogenized faecal material $\left(-80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ .
Faecal calprotectin measurement. Faecal calprotectin concentrations were determined using the fCAL ELISA Kit (Bühlmann). For patients with PSC or PSC– IBD, analyses were performed on fresh faecal samples; for patients with IBD and FGFP participants, analyses were performed on frozen faecal material $\left(-80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ .
Microbiota phylogenetic profiling. DNA extraction and sequencing data preprocessing. For the study cohort, amplicon sequencing of FGFP and patient faecal microbiota was described previously2,10. Faecal DNA extraction and microbiota profiling were performed as described previously10. Briefly, DNA was extracted from faecal material using the MoBio PowerMicrobiome RNA isolation kit with the addition of $10\mathrm{min}$ of incubation at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after the initial vortex step. The V4 region of the 16S rRNA gene was amplified with the primer pair $515\mathrm{F}/806\mathrm{R}^{27}$ . Sequencing was performed on the Illumina MiSeq platform, to generate paired-end reads of 250 base pairs in length in each direction. After demultiplexing, sequences were merged using $\mathrm{FLASH^{28}}$ software with default parameters. Successfully combined reads were filtered based on quality $(>90\%$ of nucleotides with a quality score of $\geq30$ for every read) and chimaeras removed using USEARCH (version $9.2.64)^{29}$ . For the validation cohort, faecal samples were processed altering the protocol above to dual-index barcoding as described by Tito and colleagues30. Preprocessing was performed using the DADA231 pipeline v1.6.0.
Relative microbiome profiling. For the relative microbiome matrix, each sample was downsized to 10,000 reads by random selection of reads. Samples with $<10,000$ reads were excluded (2 samples). The taxonomy of reads was assigned using RDP classifier $\mathbf{v}2.12^{32}$ .
Relative microbiome sequence variant profiling. Characterization of the microbiota profiles below the genus level was performed using the DADA231 pipeline sequence variants, with taxonomy assignment by RDP classifier $\mathbf{v}2.12^{32}$ . A phylogenetic tree for the sequence variants was reconstructed using the DADA2 sequence variant sequences, by maximum likelihood reconstruction (GTR model) using PhyML34. Sequence variants were grouped into species clusters by collapsing sequence variants with $<0.005$ branch length distance in the sequence variants tree. Competition between species clusters with increasing inflammation levels was evaluated by assessing the correlation between calprotectin levels and species clusters dominance, that is, their relative proportion of the total genus abundance per sample, excluding samples for which the genus total abundance sum was zero.
Host profiling. Serum analysis for CRP and ALP. CRP (a biomarker for systemic inflammation) and ALP (a biomarker for cholestasis in PSC) serum concentrations were extracted from the clinical panels of the patients and mHCs.
Statistical analysis. Statistical analyses were performed in R using the packages vegan35, phyloseq36, $\mathrm{FSA}^{37}$ , $\mathrm{coin^{38}}$ , DirichletMultinomial39 and fitdistrplus40. All statistical tests used were two-sided. All $P$ values were corrected for multiple testing using the Benjamini–Hochberg method (reported as the false-discovery rate-adjusted P value (FDR)) when multiple tests were performed on lists $(n>1)$ of features (for example, taxa–metadata or taxa–taxa associations) and also when performing multiple pairwise group $(n>2)$ comparisons (for example, Kruskal– Wallis test with post hoc Dunn test).
Faecal microbiome-derived features and visualization. Observed genus richness was calculated on the relative microbiome profiling matrix (downsized to 10,000 reads as described above) using phyloseq36. Enterotyping (or community typing) based on the DMM approach was performed in R as described previously14. Enterotyping was performed on a combined genus-level abundance matrix including PSC/ IBD and mHC samples compiled with 1,054 samples originating from the FGFP2. Microbiome inter-individual variation was visualized by principal coordinates analysis using Bray–Curtis dissimilarity on the genus-level abundance matrix.
Microbiome and physiological features associations. Taxa unclassified at the genus level or present in ${<}20\%$ of samples were excluded from the statistical analyses. Pearson or Spearman correlations were used for linear or rank-order correlations, respectively, between continuous variables, including genera abundances, microbial loads and levels of CRP, calprotectin and ALP. The Mann–Whitney $U$ -test was used to test median differences of continuous variables between two different groups. For more than two groups, the Kruskal–Wallis test with post hoc Dunn test was used. Statistical differences in the proportions of categorical variables (enterotypes) between patient groups were evaluated using pairwise $\chi^{2}$ tests.
Reporting Summary. Further information on research design is available in the Nature Research Reporting Summary linked to this article.
Raw amplicon sequencing data that support the findings of this study have been deposited at the European Genome-phenome Archive (EGA), with accession no. EGAS00001003600. The genus-level QMP matrix can be downloaded at http:// raeslab.org/software/QMP2/.
